WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.930
Two products sit on the shelf at the same price, and one of them carries a single word: "natural." Your hand reaches for it without a second thought.

2
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:13.860
But snake venom is natural too, and so is smallpox.

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.350
So why do we trust this word so much?

4
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.000
Imagine you're walking down the aisles of a supermarket.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.010
In your hands are two bottles of fruit juice.

6
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.300
They cost the same, and what's inside them is almost identical.

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.870
But one label shows a green leaf and a single word in large letters: natural.

8
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.720
Which one goes into your basket?

9
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.730
Most of us decide without thinking.

10
00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.220
The hand drifts on its own toward the bottle with the leaf.

11
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.230
Yet that word told us nothing at all.

12
00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.960
It didn't say what was in the bottle, how much sugar it held, or whether it would do us any good.

13
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.810
Still, it made a promise.

14
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.460
And that is exactly where the word's power lies.

15
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.710
To understand why this word is so powerful, let's first look at how vague it is.

16
00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:20.040
In the United States, the agency that regulates food has for years avoided giving the word "natural" any official definition.

17
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:30.890
All it has is a loose policy it has followed for a long time: if nothing artificial or synthetic has been added to a product that you wouldn't normally expect to find in it, the word is tolerated.

18
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:40.940
In other words, all the associations that spring to mind when someone reads the label, cleanliness, purity, health, trust, rest on no legal guarantee whatsoever.

19
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.590
We add those associations ourselves.

20
00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.120
Now consider this.

21
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.930
Arsenic is natural; it's found in soil and in rock.

22
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:58.580
Botulinum toxin, one of the most potent poisons known, is produced by a bacterium, not invented in a laboratory.

23
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:03.670
The hemlock Socrates drank when he was executed in Athens came from a plant.

24
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:11.640
Aflatoxin, released by molds that grow on peanuts and grains, ranks among the most powerful cancer-causing substances there are.

25
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:17.690
Meanwhile, the insulin that keeps millions of people alive is today largely made in laboratories.

26
00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:25.180
Eyeglasses are not natural, vaccines are not natural, and neither is the sterile glove in the operating room.

27
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.470
Yet none of us sees these things as hiding some evil inside.

28
00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.920
So why does that word on the label still seem so innocent?

29
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.170
The answer lies in several meanings stacked on top of one another within the word itself.

30
00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:51.940
"Natural" sometimes means "untouched by human hands," sometimes "ordinary, expected," and sometimes "the way things ought to be." In everyday speech we slip easily back and forth between these meanings.

31
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.550
When we say "it's natural for a child to cry," we mean it's normal.

32
00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.520
When we say "natural yogurt," we mean it has no additives.

33
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:06.730
But when we say "this relationship isn't natural," we're passing judgment, implying that something is wrong.

34
00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.060
The word's magic is hidden in that shift.

35
00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.630
A word that describes how something is quietly turns into a word that says how something ought to be.

36
00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.920
Philosophy has long looked at this shift with suspicion.

37
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:30.130
The Scottish philosopher David Hume, in A Treatise of Human Nature, which began appearing in seventeen thirty-nine, makes a striking observation about the moral writers of his day.

38
00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:37.940
For a while these writers would describe how things are, and then, without any warning to the reader, begin telling us how things ought to be.

39
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:46.870
Sentences built on "is" suddenly became sentences built on "ought." To Hume, this transition was a gap crying out for explanation.

40
00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.360
How did one leap from fact to value?

41
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.490
Later generations would remember this question as Hume's guillotine.

42
00:03:54.490 --> 00:04:05.180
Roughly two hundred years later, in nineteen oh-three, the English philosopher George Edward Moore gave a name to a similar error in his book Principia Ethica: the naturalistic fallacy.

43
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.350
What Moore meant was something more technical.

44
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:17.280
He argued that it was a mistake to try to define the concept of "good" in terms of some natural property, such as pleasure or evolutionary progress.

45
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.650
Over time the name took on a broader meaning and became the general label for the idea that "what is natural is good."

46
00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.300
But here we should pause and point out an injustice.

47
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:33.350
Treating what is "in accordance with nature" as good is not a trick dreamed up by advertisers.

48
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:39.400
Some of humanity's most deeply rooted moral traditions were built on precisely this idea.

49
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:48.490
If we brush them aside without taking them seriously and simply declare that the word "natural" is empty, we'll never understand the hold the word has on people.

50
00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:57.100
In the early third century before the common era, a merchant from Cyprus, Zeno of Citium, began teaching in a painted colonnade in Athens.

51
00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:01.070
The school took its name from that colonnade: the Stoa.

52
00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:08.880
According to ancient sources, Zeno summed up the purpose of human life in a single phrase: to live in agreement with nature.

53
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.850
If we read those words in today's sense, we get them wrong.

54
00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.620
The Stoics were not telling people to return to the forest or abandon the city.

55
00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.910
To them, the universe was a whole ordered by reason.

56
00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:31.480
Chrysippus, regarded as the school's most prolific thinker, says that nature has two layers: the nature of the universe as a whole, and the nature of the human being.

57
00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.650
And human nature is reason.

58
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.900
So living according to nature did not mean chasing after anger, fear and ambition.

59
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.190
Quite the opposite: it meant passing them through the filter of reason.

60
00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:52.480
Centuries later, in Rome, Epictetus, a freed slave, gave this idea a form that sits very close to everyday life.

61
00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.290
Some things are within our power, and some are not.

62
00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:02.820
Whether it rains, whether someone insults us, illness, death: none of these are within our power.

63
00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.310
How we respond to them, however, is.

64
00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:16.040
From the Stoic point of view, living in accordance with nature means yielding to what we cannot change, and taking responsibility for what we can change, namely our own judgment.

65
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:20.330
Here, "nature" does not describe a given state of things.

66
00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:23.340
It describes a discipline.

67
00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.590
The Stoic who lives in accordance with nature is someone who has learned to rein himself in.

68
00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:34.640
At the other end of the world, in China, a similar search was unfolding in an entirely different language.

69
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:44.050
The Tao Te Ching, attributed to Laozi, which means the Book of the Way and of Virtue, warns against the person who tries to rein in the universe with boundaries and rules.

70
00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:53.460
In the twenty-fifth chapter of the book, a ladder is set up: humanity takes the earth as its model, the earth takes heaven, and heaven takes the Way, that is, the Tao.

71
00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.910
The Tao, in turn, takes "ziran" as its model.

72
00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:10.040
Although dictionaries translate the word ziran as "nature," its real meaning is "that which is so of itself." The Taoist sage is the one who does not interfere with the natural flow of things, who acts without forcing.

73
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:13.690
This is called "wu wei": acting without forcing.

74
00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:18.460
The thinker who conveys this idea through the most vivid examples is Zhuangzi.

75
00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:24.030
In one of his stories, a famous horse trainer takes horses from their mountain pastures.

76
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:30.880
He clips their manes, pares their hooves, brands them, bridles them, and shuts them away in stables.

77
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.650
In the end, some of the horses cannot endure this training, and they die.

78
00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:40.420
Elsewhere, Zhuangzi reminds us that the duck's legs are short and the crane's are long.

79
00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.990
Try to lengthen the duck's legs or shorten the crane's, and you will bring suffering to both.

80
00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.800
Here again, "the natural" is not a given state of things.

81
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:55.210
It is a warning that the proper measure of each being must not be distorted by artificial molds.

82
00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:05.580
In Islamic thought, the answer to these questions is the concept of "fitra." The thirtieth verse of the Surah ar-Rum speaks of the fitra upon which God created human beings.

83
00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:15.310
A well-known hadith opens up the idea further: every child is born upon the fitra, and it is the parents who make the child a Jew, a Christian, or a Zoroastrian.

84
00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:23.120
Fitra is a fundamental disposition placed within the human being, an inner constitution inclined to turn toward truth and toward the good.

85
00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:34.450
Toward the end of the eleventh century, al-Ghazali recounts a memory from his youth in a work that is a kind of intellectual autobiography, al-Munqidh min al-Dalal, or Deliverance from Error.

86
00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:43.860
He writes that he noticed how the children of Christian families grew up Christian, the children of Jewish families grew up Jewish, and the children of Muslim families grew up Muslim.

87
00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:51.510
This observation, together with the hadith he remembered, led him to separate belief acquired through imitation from a person's true fitra.

88
00:08:51.510 --> 00:09:02.360
For al-Ghazali, fitra is not something about which we can say, "it is already within us, and therefore it is right." We can only bring it to light by scraping away the layers of habit that cover it.

89
00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.410
Notice something here.

90
00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:12.540
These three traditions, the Stoa, the Tao, and the idea of fitra, come from very different places, yet they do something in common.

91
00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:17.950
None of them treats "the natural" as the same thing as whatever you happen to crave in the moment.

92
00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.600
For the Stoic, nature is reason.

93
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.530
For the Taoist, it is flowing without distorting one's own measure.

94
00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:32.100
And for al-Ghazali, it is a starting point that has been covered over and must be rediscovered.

95
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:37.350
In all three, nature serves as a compass, but reading that compass takes effort.

96
00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.080
The label on the supermarket shelf, by contrast, promises to let us skip that effort.

97
00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:50.090
These ancient traditions looked at nature with the conviction that within all of it lay a certain goodness and order.

98
00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:58.860
And the question an English philosopher would ask in the nineteenth century would take aim at precisely that conviction: what if that order is not as good as we believe?

99
00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:05.230
In the eighteen-fifties, during the most productive years of his life, John Stuart Mill wrote a long essay.

100
00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:08.400
Its title was a single word: Nature.

101
00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.890
Mill did not publish this text during his lifetime.

102
00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:21.460
The essay appeared in eighteen seventy-four, one year after Mill's death in eighteen seventy-three, published as a book together with two other essays he had written on religion.

103
00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:27.190
The essay on Nature reads like an indictment aimed at the counsel to "live according to nature."

104
00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.360
Mill begins by taking the concept apart.

105
00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:34.490
The word "nature," he says, can mean two things.

106
00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:41.180
The first is everything that exists: all events, all forces, and the human being included among them.

107
00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:47.710
In this sense, the counsel to "follow nature" says nothing at all, because no one can step outside of nature.

108
00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:52.640
There is no thief who breaks the laws of physics, no liar who defies gravity.

109
00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.650
Everyone already conforms to nature.

110
00:10:55.650 --> 00:11:01.700
The second meaning of the word is whatever happens of its own accord, without human intervention.

111
00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:07.430
In this sense, Mill says, the counsel is not meaningless, but it is irrational and immoral.

112
00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:14.120
It is irrational, because every human action amounts to altering the spontaneous course of nature.

113
00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:21.450
Building a house, raising a bridge, even opening an umbrella to shelter from the rain: all of these are such acts.

114
00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:27.340
If we are to take nature as our model, then plowing a field or binding a wound would be wrong as well.

115
00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:31.630
He describes its immorality in the most unsettling part of the essay.

116
00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:41.360
We might paraphrase Mill roughly like this: nearly everything for which people are hanged or imprisoned when they do it to one another, nature does every single day.

117
00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:51.410
Nature impales people, has them torn apart by wild beasts, burns them in fire, crushes them with stones, starves them, freezes them, poisons them.

118
00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:57.620
And in doing so, it makes no distinction between good and evil, between the guilty and the innocent.

119
00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:02.550
An earthquake leaves the murderer and the mother nursing her child beneath the same rubble.

120
00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:06.520
An epidemic never asks anyone what they have done.

121
00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:14.170
If a human being did such things, Mill argues, we would not hesitate for a moment to call them by their proper name.

122
00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:18.460
So why should we look to such a nature as our teacher of morals?

123
00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:21.950
Mill was not alone in this outburst in his day.

124
00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:30.880
Around the same time, in eighteen sixty, Charles Darwin wrote a letter to the American botanist Asa Gray in which he mentions a family of insects.

125
00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:39.170
The parasitic wasps of this family lay their eggs inside the bodies of caterpillars, and the larvae grow by eating the caterpillar alive from within.

126
00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:46.500
Darwin writes that he cannot persuade himself that a beneficent creator would have designed these creatures expressly for that purpose.

127
00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:52.550
For both Darwin and Mill, the worry was the price of treating nature's order as a moral model.

128
00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:57.320
Anyone who looked at nature closely came face to face with suffering beyond measure.

129
00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.330
Mill's indictment does not end there.

130
00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:06.220
His real target is the way the idea that "what is natural is good" plays out in everyday life.

131
00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:09.870
He looks at the impulses that arise in us spontaneously.

132
00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:15.600
A child, he says, is not born generous, patient or honest.

133
00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.290
These things are learned.

134
00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:25.460
Even cleanliness, in his view, is not a natural inclination but a habit acquired through education.

135
00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:29.270
Courage is won through a struggle against fear.

136
00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:35.960
For Mill, nearly all the human virtues worthy of praise emerge from disciplining what nature hands us ready-made.

137
00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:41.690
Seen this way, humanity's moral task is not to imitate nature, but to correct it.

138
00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:45.500
Let us pause here for a moment and return to the Stoics.

139
00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.710
Did Mill really refute them?

140
00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:49.760
Not entirely.

141
00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:53.730
As we saw earlier, for Chrysippus human nature was reason.

142
00:13:53.730 --> 00:14:04.900
What Mill called "correcting," the Stoic described as "living in accordance with nature." The two sides were saying almost the same thing in different words: do not leave a spontaneous impulse as it is;

143
00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.430
give it shape through reason.

144
00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:12.040
Mill's real objection was to how loosely the word "nature" was being used.

145
00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:21.770
If a single word can mean "reason," "instinct," "the ordinary" and "what ought to be" all at once, then anyone can win an argument simply by concealing which meaning they are using.

146
00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:25.100
Mill's concern was not unfounded.

147
00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:29.710
History is full of examples of how this word can be put to use.

148
00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:35.280
In his work the Politics, Aristotle argued that some people are slaves by nature.

149
00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:45.970
For centuries, keeping women out of politics and education was defended on the grounds that it would be "contrary to their nature." In many schools, the hands of left-handed children were tied,

150
00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.300
because their condition was seen as a natural deviation.

151
00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:54.070
In each of these examples, "nature" was not being used to explain anything.

152
00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:58.360
It was being used to place an existing order beyond question.

153
00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:06.170
When you ground something in nature, anyone who objects to it seems to be standing not just against you, but against the universe itself.

154
00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:10.940
And so Mill's essay leaves the reader alone with an unsettling question.

155
00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:18.270
If the word "natural" does not really describe anything, why is it laid on the table like a trump card at the end of every argument?

156
00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:22.880
The question cuts deepest when it is asked about human behavior itself.

157
00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:30.370
"It's human nature." How many times have we heard that sentence, and how many times have we said it?

158
00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:40.580
When someone cannot rein in their anger, when someone is unfaithful, when a community looks upon a stranger with hostility, when the strong crush the weak, that sentence is always there.

159
00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.030
Sometimes it is said with a sigh, sometimes with a shrug.

160
00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:50.120
On the surface it is an explanation, but more often than not it serves as a full stop.

161
00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:55.530
The moment it is spoken, the discussion ends, because what can anyone do against nature?

162
00:15:55.530 --> 00:16:01.900
Today this sentence is even more powerful than before, because it now speaks the language of science as well.

163
00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:13.070
Evolutionary biology, and the psychology that leans on it, look for the origins of aggression, jealousy, in-group favoritism and the pull toward short-term pleasure in the conditions our ancestors lived in.

164
00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:16.560
Much of this research is serious and valuable.

165
00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:21.170
Understanding where a behavior comes from also helps us find ways to change it.

166
00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:25.940
The trouble begins the moment a finding makes its way into everyday conversation.

167
00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:37.590
As it passes from ear to ear, the sentence "this tendency may have an evolutionary origin" becomes "this tendency is natural." And the next step is always the same: "If it's natural,

168
00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:46.040
there's nothing to be done." The very gap Hume pointed out two hundred and seventy years ago is crossed once again, this time wearing a lab coat.

169
00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:52.410
There is a sadness in this, because the slide usually comes not from bad intent but from exhaustion.

170
00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:55.900
We all know how hard it is to change ourselves.

171
00:16:55.900 --> 00:17:00.030
How many times have we resolved to break a habit and failed?

172
00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:05.600
How many times, after a flash of anger, have we said, "That's just how I am"?

173
00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:09.730
The phrase "it's human nature" lightens that burden.

174
00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:16.420
It lifts the blame from the individual and places it on the species, even on a past stretching back millions of years.

175
00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:23.590
In one sense, it is a compassionate phrase: it keeps us from being merciless toward ourselves.

176
00:17:23.590 --> 00:17:28.680
But in another sense, it also frees us from being accountable to ourselves.

177
00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:37.450
The phrase has a flip side, too: "unnatural." Saying that something is not natural carries a verdict every bit as absolute as saying that it is.

178
00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:46.060
To describe a way of life, a choice, a technology or a treatment as "against nature" is to condemn it before any discussion begins.

179
00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:53.710
This was exactly the danger Mill warned about: whichever way the word is turned, it lets us reach a judgment without offering a reason.

180
00:17:53.710 --> 00:18:02.320
Seen with an impartial eye, whether we say "natural" or "unnatural," the question that needs asking stays the same: Is this thing good or bad?

181
00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:05.650
Does it harm people, or does it benefit them?

182
00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:10.740
The answer to those questions cannot be found by asking whether the thing comes from nature.

183
00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:14.550
So does nature tell us nothing at all about morality?

184
00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:18.520
That, too, would be too harsh a conclusion.

185
00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:23.930
Perhaps the most balanced answer was given long before Mill, once again by Aristotle.

186
00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:30.780
In the second book of his Nicomachean Ethics, he writes that the virtues arise in us neither by nature nor against nature.

187
00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:36.830
Nature gives us the capacity to receive the virtues, and it is habit that brings them to completion.

188
00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:42.400
To be brave, we must act bravely; to be just, we must act justly.

189
00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:48.930
And if we remember that this same Aristotle grounded slavery in nature, the line becomes all the more meaningful.

190
00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:55.460
A single thinker left us, within the same body of work, both the most dangerous and the wisest use of the word.

191
00:18:55.460 --> 00:19:03.750
The Roman orator Cicero, too, calls habit "a kind of second nature." The expression still lives in our language: we say,

192
00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:12.520
"That job has become second nature to him." Perhaps the way to break the spell of the word "natural" lies hidden in this second nature.

193
00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:16.170
Our first nature is a starting point we were given.

194
00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:22.380
The urges, inclinations, fears and desires that arise on their own are part of that beginning.

195
00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.750
We did not choose them.

196
00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:32.720
But our second nature, the character we acquire through repetition and choice, through reflection and trial, is something we can choose.

197
00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:41.170
Al-Ghazali's effort to scrape away the layers of imitation covering our innate nature, the Stoic's passing of his anger through the filter of reason,

198
00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:48.500
even Zhuangzi's warning that every being should keep to its own measure, all of them tell us that this second nature is built through labor.

199
00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:57.750
Today the word "natural" is used more often than ever before, in the supermarket, at the pharmacy, in political debates and around family dinner tables.

200
00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:04.440
Sometimes it sells a product, sometimes it excuses a behavior, and sometimes it shuts a person out.

201
00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:10.010
The reason we love the word so much may be that it spares us the hardest question: Is this good?

202
00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:16.700
Asking that question demands thinking, weighing, and sometimes confronting our own habits.

203
00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:21.790
"Natural," on the other hand, arrives with the answer already in hand.

204
00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:29.280
That little green leaf on the label may be promising exactly this: that we can reach what is good without going to the trouble of thinking.

205
00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:32.610
Perhaps we don't need to abandon the word altogether.

206
00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:38.980
It may be enough to ask a small question wherever we hear it: Natural in what sense?

207
00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:45.670
Because it happens on its own, because it is ordinary, or because someone wants to tell us that this is how things ought to be?

208
00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:52.040
Once we start asking that question, the word does not lose its power; it only loses its spell.

209
00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:57.930
What remains is a starting point, and from that point on, we decide for ourselves what to do.

210
00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:01.900
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

211
00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:04.430
Until we meet again in a new story.
